'''''Oxalis versicolor''''' or '''candy cane sorrel''' is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the [[family (botany)|family]] Oxalidaceae found in [[South Africa]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tropicos.org/Name/23700878|title= ''Oxalis versicolor'' |publisher=Tropicos}}</ref>

A [[bulb]]ous [[perennial plant|perennial]], it grows to {{convert|8-15 |cm|in|abbr=on}} forming a mound of fresh green leaves, each leaf composed of three elongated leaflets. In late summer and autumn, narrow white tubular buds form at the tip of slender stems. A curved scarlet edging to each petal gives the appearance of a [[candy cane]]. The flowers open in full sunlight, but remain furled at other times.<ref>{{cite web | title = Oxalis versicolor (candy cane sorrel) | url = https://www.gardenia.net/plant/Oxalis-versicolor
| publisher = Gardenia.net | access-date = 15 April 2018}}</ref>

In cultivation in the UK this plant is only completely hardy in mild or coastal areas, down to {{convert|-5|C|F|abbr=on}}. It has been given the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web | url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/42157/i-Oxalis-versicolor-i/Details | title = RHS Plantfinder – ''Oxalis versicolor'' | access-date = 14 April 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants – Ornamental | date = July 2017 | page = 70 | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 14 April 2018}}</ref>

In the United States this plant grows up to 12 inches tall, prefers full to partial sun, blooms in the summer, and is hardy in USDA zones 7 – 9.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Spring 2021 Garden Catalog|publisher=Burgess Seed & Plant Co.|year=2021|location=Bloomington, IL|pages=20}}</ref>

